<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tögs-Ochiryn Namnansüren</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Namnansüren, Sain Noyon Khan Namnansüren · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tögs-Ochiryn Namnansüren, the Sain Noyon Khan, was a Mongolian prince who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> nobles in the independence movement of 1911. He served as prime minister of the Bogd Khanate from 1912 until his death in 1919, and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1913 to seek recognition and support for Mongolian independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/togs-ochiryn-namnansuren.jpg" alt="Tögs-Ochiryn Namnansüren" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scarlet.Waffen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yagaan_noyon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Namnansüren was born in 1878 in what is now Uyanga district of Övörkhangai Province. He descended from Onokhui Üizen Noyan, third son of Gersenji Khongtaiji of the Jalayir, who was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a> and so a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. In 1896 he became prince of Sain Noyon Khan Province, one of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> provinces set up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, as the 13th holder of a hereditary title traced to Tumenkhen Sain Noyon in the early 17th century. The Qing court had raised his ancestor Tsering to the rank of khan in 1728, separating the domain from the Tusheet Khanate. He married in 1900.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1911 Namnansüren persuaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogd-khan">Bogd Khan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>'s religious leader, to call a congress of princes and high lamas in Khüree to begin the break from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and in July 1911 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> with a delegation seeking Russian and Western European support. The Bogd Khan named him prime minister in July 1912, replacing Da Lam Tserenchimed, who as Minister of Internal Affairs had acted as head of government since December 1911.</p><p>From November 1913 to January 1914 he led a second delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> during the Russian and Chinese negotiations that produced the tripartite Kyakhta treaty. The agreement reconfirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>'s status as an autonomous region within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, ending hopes of recognised independence and of union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>. Russian officials stopped his attempts to contact the ambassadors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and to travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, and his influence had waned when he came home in 1914.</p><p>In June 1918, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> demanding that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> give up its Pan-Mongolia ambitions and renounce its autonomy, he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irkutsk">Irkutsk</a>. There he met two Bolshevik representatives, in what is believed to be the first meeting between Soviet and Outer Mongolian officials, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, absorbed by the revolution and the civil war, offered little help.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Namnansüren fell seriously ill soon after his return and died in April 1919. Many suspected that he had been poisoned, as several other figures of the revival of Mongolian independence also died early. Shortly afterwards the Chinese warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-shuzheng">Xu Shuzheng</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Niislel Khüree</a> and installed Gonchigjalzangiin Badamdorj as prime minister. In 1914 Namnansüren had brought films from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to show at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogd-khan">Bogd Khan</a>'s residence, the first known film showings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tögs-Ochiryn Namnansüren” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was a theocratic monarchy with ministries headed by nobles and lamas, among them the prime minister <b>Sain Noyon Khan Namnansüren</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bogd-khanate-of-mongolia">Bogd Khanate of Mongolia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Uyanga, in what is now <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.46,102.28&amp;year=1904" aria-label="Uyanga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6959 530 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tögs-Ochiryn Namnansüren"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7409 800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7409 800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.46,102.28&amp;year=1904">What was here in 1904? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
